  "query_preview": "..\n\nA longwave trough is situated over the Great Lakes this morning with \nan embedded midlevel shortwave exiting toward Lake Ontario. At the \nsurface, the cold front that passed through last night is now midway \nacross Lake Erie. Thermal change behind this front is relatively \nweak, and guidance supports another seasonable day with highs near \n80. Diurnal instability will be stifled by mid-level warming via \npost-wave subsidence, but enough low-level moisture will exist for a \nhealthy flare-up of cumulus. A few shallow late afternoon and \nevening showers will also be possible, mainly over the Thumb where \nveering northerly flow off the lake provides some convergence to act \nas a trigger.\n\nA backdoor cold front marking a much drier air mass will arrive this \nevening and precede high pressure that settles in on Friday. Light \nnortherly wind allows dew points to settle into the 50s, offering \nvery pleasant and less humid conditions that continue into Saturday. \nBy Saturday afternoon, a slight southwest breeze develops as the \nsurface ridge settles east. A weak disturbance working into the \nregion later in the day leads to fgen and some convection near a \ndeveloping warm front, but this is favored to set up over northern \nMI so have maintained a dry forecast.\n\nA warming trend remains on track for early next week as a \nsubtropical ridge migrates from the Four Corners into the southern \nPlains and eventually the Tennessee Valley. Thickness advection \ncommences Sunday, pushing temps above average, then strengthening \nsouthwest flow north of the ridge advects 850mb temps around 20C or \nabove for Monday and Tuesday. NBM and LREF ensemble guidance \ncontinue to advertise 25-50% probability for highs in excess of 90 \ndegrees each day, with highest likelihood over the urban corridor. \nThe caveat for the heat forecast will be potential for periodic MCS \nactivity as several ridge-running shortwaves are shown in mid-level \nflow during this period. A cold front on Tuesday or Wednesday looks \nto break the heat and may bring a more focused signal for convection.",
